From 19b01e3f02670d9f3d10f057f30908ca90f64691 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Holdren <Dylan@UniversalBasicInc.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:24:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Run neural network potentials on Intel GPUs through LibTorch
 XPU

The NNPot interface asked only the CUDA or HIP runtime for a device, so
with a LibTorch built for Intel GPUs the model always ran on the CPU.
When the LibTorch found at configure time has XPU support (it exports
the torch_xpu target), TorchModel now runs the model on the first Intel
GPU LibTorch enumerates, both by default and with GMX_NN_DEVICE=gpu;
which GPUs LibTorch sees can be restricted with ONEAPI_DEVICE_SELECTOR
or ZE_AFFINITY_MASK. SYCL has no process-wide current device, so unlike
the CUDA and HIP paths the device is not inherited from mdrun; passing
the device of the rank to the module is left to a follow-up (#5467).

CMake warns when a SYCL build for Intel GPUs is paired with a LibTorch
without XPU support. The NNPot unit tests also run the GPU cases on
Intel devices.

Refs #5754
---
 cmake/gmxManageNNPot.cmake                    | 11 +++++
 docs/install-guide/index.rst                  |  7 +++
 docs/reference-manual/special/nnpot.rst       | 10 +++++
 docs/release-notes/2027/major/features.rst    | 11 +++++
 src/config.h.cmakein                          |  3 ++
 .../nnpot/tests/nnpotforceprovider.cpp        | 16 ++++---
 .../applied_forces/nnpot/torchmodel.cpp       | 43 +++++++++++++++++--
 7 files changed, 91 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/cmake/gmxManageNNPot.cmake b/cmake/gmxManageNNPot.cmake
index 2302180..4bcb504 100644
--- a/cmake/gmxManageNNPot.cmake
+++ b/cmake/gmxManageNNPot.cmake
@@ -82,6 +82,17 @@ if(NOT GMX_NNPOT STREQUAL "OFF")
                                 "compiled with the cxx11 ABI, which is required for building GROMACS.")
         endif()
 
+        # A LibTorch built with XPU support (Intel GPUs, PyTorch >= 2.5) exports the torch_xpu target
+        set(GMX_TORCH_XPU OFF)
+        if(TARGET torch_xpu)
+            set(GMX_TORCH_XPU ON)
+        endif()
+        if(GMX_GPU_SYCL AND GMX_SYCL_DPCPP AND GMX_DPCPP_HAVE_INTEL_TARGET AND NOT GMX_TORCH_XPU AND NOT FIND_TORCH_QUIETLY)
+            message(WARNING "GROMACS is built for Intel GPUs with SYCL, but the LibTorch found has no XPU "
+                            "support, so neural network potential inference will run on the CPU. Use a "
+                            "LibTorch with XPU support (PyTorch >= 2.5) to run the model on the GPU.")
+        endif()
+
         set(GMX_TORCH ON)
     elseif(GMX_NNPOT STREQUAL "TORCH")
         message(FATAL_ERROR "Torch not found. Please install libtorch and add its installation prefix"
diff --git a/docs/install-guide/index.rst b/docs/install-guide/index.rst
index 775b0da..2a3bee3 100644
--- a/docs/install-guide/index.rst
+++ b/docs/install-guide/index.rst
@@ -1466,6 +1466,13 @@ You must use the same ABI version as you use when building the rest of
 So get (or build) the CXX11 ABI version of Libtorch.
 For the same reason, it is also not possible to use the Libtorch version that ships
 with a conda installation of Pytorch, because it is built with the pre-CXX11 ABI by default.
+To run model inference on Intel GPUs, use a Libtorch with XPU support (PyTorch 2.5 or
+later, for example the one bundled in the ``torch`` XPU wheel). |Gromacs| itself can be
+built with or without SYCL, but the CMake configuration of such a Libtorch requires an
+Intel oneAPI DPC++ compiler installation at configure time (found through ``SYCL_ROOT``
+or ``CMPLR_ROOT``, which sourcing ``setvars.sh`` sets, or at
+``/opt/intel/oneapi/compiler/latest``), and the oneAPI runtime libraries that Libtorch
+links against must be on the library path at run time.
 The NNP interface is enabled by default when a Libtorch installation is found in the
 ``CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH``, or ``Torch_DIR`` is set to a ``TorchConfig.cmake`` or
 ``torch-config.cmake`` usually found under ``share/cmake/Torch/`` in the libtorch
diff --git a/docs/reference-manual/special/nnpot.rst b/docs/reference-manual/special/nnpot.rst
index e5b7ea9..a119518 100644
--- a/docs/reference-manual/special/nnpot.rst
+++ b/docs/reference-manual/special/nnpot.rst
@@ -68,6 +68,16 @@ is deprecated, and is treated as ``gpu``.
 If multiple GPUs are available, the active device will be inherited from mdrun.
 For ``gpu``, a CUDA/HIP-aware LibTorch installation should be installed, and the
 corresponding CUDA/HIP installation should be available in your ``PATH``.
+On Intel GPUs, a LibTorch with XPU support (PyTorch 2.5 or later) is needed. Unlike
+for CUDA and HIP, the device is not inherited from mdrun: the model runs on the first
+Intel GPU LibTorch enumerates, which can be restricted with ``ONEAPI_DEVICE_SELECTOR``
+or ``ZE_AFFINITY_MASK``. When |Gromacs| itself is built with SYCL, two things apply at
+run time. First, |Gromacs| and LibTorch must use one and the same SYCL runtime
+(``libsycl``): build |Gromacs| with the oneAPI release whose runtime the LibTorch
+package bundles, or make sure the runtime bundled with LibTorch is the one loaded;
+with two different runtimes in the process either side can crash. Second, expose a
+single SYCL backend, e.g. ``ONEAPI_DEVICE_SELECTOR=level_zero:gpu``, as |Gromacs|
+itself recommends when it detects a GPU through more than one backend.
 
 Find below an example Python code snippet to export a pretrained model in
 PyTorch using TorchScript. As an example, we use the popular ANI models
